HTTPS

Local HTTPS

The project can use a manually created local certificate.

Recommended files:

.cert/
  localhost-key.pem
  localhost.pem

The certificate files are local development secrets and should not be committed.

Add .cert/ to .gitignore.

A local self-signed certificate can still produce a browser warning. That is normal for an untrusted local certificate.

Production HTTPS

Production HTTPS is provided by the hosting/CDN/domain setup. Do not deploy the local self-signed certificate.

Recommended production setup:

Domain
  ↓
HTTPS/TLS
  ↓
CDN / WAF
  ↓
Static hosting
  ↓
dist/

Error checks

Build

npm run build

A clean build should finish with:

✓ built in ...

Lint

npm run lint

Browser errors

Open:

F12 → Console

Ignore browser-extension errors that clearly come from extension files such as:

contentscript.js
ObjectMultiplex
app-init-liveness
background-liveness

Those are not evidence of an application error unless the same issue appears in the site's own source/runtime stack.

Image check

find public/images -maxdepth 1 -type f | sort

Then compare every physical image filename with src/data/images.js.

A successful Vite build does not guarantee that a referenced public image actually exists, so runtime image checking is still required.

Production safety

The frontend is static, so do not expose the Vite development server as production.

For production use:

HTTPS/TLS

CDN

caching

WAF/DDoS protection offered by the hosting provider

appropriate hosting limits/rate controls

These are deployment-layer protections, not React code protections.

